{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "def max_sum(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence in the given array.\n    A bitonic subsequence first strictly increases and then strictly decreases.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # lis_sum[i] stores the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence ending at arr[i]\n    lis_sum = list(arr)\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                lis_sum[i] = max(lis_sum[i], lis_sum[j] + arr[i])\n\n    # lds_sum[i] stores the maximum sum of a decreasing subsequence starting at arr[i]\n    lds_sum = list(arr)\n    for i in range(n - 2, -1, -1):\n        for j in range(n - 1, i, -1):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                lds_sum[i] = max(lds_sum[i], lds_sum[j] + arr[i])\n\n    max_bitonic_sum = 0\n    # For each element arr[i], consider it as the peak of a bitonic subsequence\n    for i in range(n):\n        # A valid bitonic subsequence must have at least one element on each side of the peak (if it's not the first or last element)\n        # However, the problem definition implies \"first increasing THEN decreasing\", meaning a single element is not bitonic.\n        # But if the increasing or decreasing part can be empty, then it could be simpler.\n        # The example implies a sum of 194 for [1, 15, 51, 45, 33, 100, 12, 18, 9] which is 1 + 15 + 51 + 100 + 18 + 9\n        # In this specific case, 100 is a peak, 1, 15, 51 are increasing part and 18, 9 are decreasing part.\n        # This implies that the 'peak' element (arr[i]) is included in both the LIS and LDS sums.\n        # We subtract arr[i] once to avoid double counting it.\n        # Note: A pure increasing or pure decreasing sequence is also bitonic (with an empty decreasing/increasing part).\n        # We should ensure that a valid increasing part and valid decreasing part both exist, potentially just the peak itself.\n        # The current sum `lis_sum[i] + lds_sum[i] - arr[i]` works even if one part is empty (e.g., LIS part is just arr[i], LDS part is just arr[i])\n        # This covers cases where the bitonic sequence is purely increasing or purely decreasing.\n        max_bitonic_sum = max(max_bitonic_sum, lis_sum[i] + lds_sum[i] - arr[i])\n\n    return max_bitonic_sum"}
