{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(value: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Set the leftmost unset (0) bit of a non-negative integer.\n    Returns the integer with the leftmost unset bit set to 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if value < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a non-negative integer\")\n\n    if value == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # Iterate from the most significant bit (within value's current representation) down to 0.\n    # We are looking for the highest bit position 'p' where the bit is 0.\n    for p in range(value.bit_length() - 1, -1, -1):\n        # Check if the bit at position 'p' is 0\n        if not (value & (1 << p)):\n            # If it's 0, set this bit and return the new value.\n            return value | (1 << p)\n\n    # If the loop completes, it means all bits from 0 to value.bit_length() - 1 are set (e.g., 0b111).\n    # In this case, the leftmost unset bit is at the next highest position, which is value.bit_length().\n    return value | (1 << value.bit_length())"}
