{"task_id": "Mbpp/440", "solution": "import nltk\nfrom typing import Tuple\nimport re\ndef find_adverb_position(sentence: str) -> Tuple[int, int, str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the first adverb and its position in a given sentence, excluding trailing punctuation.\n    Returns a tuple (start_index, end_index, adverb_string) or an empty tuple if no adverb is found.\n    \"\"\"\n    tokens = nltk.word_tokenize(sentence)\n    tagged_tokens = nltk.pos_tag(tokens)\n\n    current_search_start_idx = 0\n    for word_token, tag in tagged_tokens:\n        token_start_idx = sentence.find(word_token, current_search_start_idx)\n        \n        # Heuristic to advance if token isn't found, preventing infinite loops in case of tokenization quirks.\n        if token_start_idx == -1:\n            current_search_start_idx += len(word_token)\n            continue\n\n        # NLTK uses 'RB' for adverbs (RB, RBR for comparative, RBS for superlative).\n        if tag.startswith('RB'):\n            # Strip common trailing punctuation from the adverb string to match example behavior.\n            trailing_punctuation = '.,!?;:\"\\''\n            clean_adverb = word_token.rstrip(trailing_punctuation)\n            \n            # Ensure the cleaned string is not empty (e.g., if token was just punctuation).\n            if not clean_adverb:\n                current_search_start_idx = token_start_idx + len(word_token)\n                continue\n\n            # Calculate the end index based on the original token's start and the cleaned adverb's length.\n            adverb_end_idx = token_start_idx + len(clean_adverb)\n            \n            return (token_start_idx, adverb_end_idx, clean_adverb)\n\n        # Advance the search index past the current token for the next iteration.\n        current_search_start_idx = token_start_idx + len(word_token)\n\n    return ()"}
