{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "def max_product(numbers: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum product formed by multiplying numbers of an increasing contiguous subarray.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return 0\n\n    n = len(numbers)\n    # dp_products[i] stores the maximum product of an increasing contiguous subarray ending at index i.\n    dp_products = [0] * n\n\n    # Initialize with the first element\n    dp_products[0] = numbers[0]\n    max_overall_product = numbers[0]\n\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        # If the current number extends the increasing contiguous subarray\n        if numbers[i] > numbers[i-1]:\n            # The product can either be the current number itself (starting a new sequence)\n            # or the product of the previous sequence extended by the current number.\n            # We take the maximum of these two to handle negative numbers correctly.\n            dp_products[i] = max(numbers[i], dp_products[i-1] * numbers[i])\n        else:\n            # If the increasing sequence breaks, the current number starts a new sequence.\n            dp_products[i] = numbers[i]\n\n        max_overall_product = max(max_overall_product, dp_products[i])\n\n    return max_overall_product"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/472", "solution": "def check_Consecutive(lst: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given list contains consecutive numbers.\n    The list is considered consecutive if, when sorted, each element is one greater than the previous.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not lst:\n        return False\n    \n    # Sort the list to easily check for consecutiveness\n    sorted_lst = sorted(lst)\n    \n    # Iterate through the sorted list and check if each element is one greater than the previous\n    for i in range(1, len(sorted_lst)):\n        if sorted_lst[i] != sorted_lst[i-1] + 1:\n            return False\n            \n    return True"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/473", "solution": "def tuple_intersection(list1, list2):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the intersection of elements in two tuple lists, ignoring tuple element order.\n    Normalizes tuples by sorting elements to handle (a, b) and (b, a) as identical.\n    \"\"\"\n    normalized_set1 = {tuple(sorted(t)) for t in list1}\n    normalized_set2 = {tuple(sorted(t)) for t in list2}\n    return normalized_set1.intersection(normalized_set2)"}
